  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Prolonged period of dry weather continues for the \nremainder of the week with a large diurnal temperature range. \nSeasonable chilly nights with lows mainly in the 20s & 30s. \nUnseasonably mild afternoons with plenty of sunshine and highs \nmainly in the 60s.\n\nAn upper level ridge of high pressure will remain in control of our \nweather for the remainder of the week. The result will be the \ncontinuation of a prolonged period of dry weather along with a large \ndiurnal temperature range. The NBM guidance appears not warm enough \nduring the afternoons and not cool enough at night with excellent \nradiational cooling conditions much of the time. Low temps will \nmainly be in the 20s and 30s...except some lower to middle 40s\nin the typically mildest non-decoupling spots which include\ndowntown Boston. Highs will mainly be in the 60s for the next\nseveral days.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...The potential exists for a windswept heavy rain\nand possibly even some coastal flooding for the start of the \nnext work week...but only if a storm tracks close enough to the\ncoast. \n\nAll attention will then turn towards vigorous southern stream \nshortwave energy in the vicinity of the Gulf and into Florida. This \nsystem will be loaded with moisture. This will combined with \nanother piece of shortwave energy moving out of the western U.S.\nThe model guidance indicates that the southern stream shortwave\nenergy may undergo strong cyclogenesis off the Florida coast. \nIf the upper level flow becomes amplified enough...this strong \nstorm would track up the coast coast and bring a windswept heavy\nrain event to kick off the next work week. In \naddition...astronomical high tides will be on the increase so \nthe potential would also exist for coastal flooding. This \npotential is supported by the latest 00z operational runs of the\nGFS/CMC/ICON models guidance. However...the 00z UKMET/ECMWF \nindicate a track much further southeast which would result in a \nglancing blow or perhaps even a complete miss. For what it is \nworth...the latest ECMWF ensembles have a significant number of \nmembers that are closer to the coast than the operational run \nincluding the AI version of the model. \n\nSo in a nutshell...the potential for a strong coastal storm to \nbring a windswept heavy rain and even the risk for coastal \nflooding is a possibility to kick off the next work week. \nHowever...there remains the possibility that this storm tracks \nfar enough east for just a glancing blow or even a complete \nmiss. While odds of a significant storm have increased over the\npast 24 hours...nothing is set in stone at this time range. \nCertainly something we will need to monitor closely over the \nnext few days.",
